      "file": "src/cfgparse.c",
      "function": "readcfgfile",
      "line": 1941,
      "code": "err = parse_line(line, outline, &outlen, args, &arg,\n                 PARSE_OPT_ENV | PARSE_OPT_DQUOTE | PARSE_OPT_SQUOTE |\n                 PARSE_OPT_BKSLASH | PARSE_OPT_SHARP, &errptr);",
      "note": "The harness has already written the witness to a temporary file and `readcfgfile()` has read the current line, stripped the trailing newline, and skipped leading spaces. Execution is now inside the configuration-line tokenizer used by the crashing path.",
      "oracle_kind": "gt_parser_checkpoint",
      "kind": "parser_admitted"
    },
    {
      "file": "src/cfgparse.c",
      "function": "readcfgfile",
      "line": 1941,
      "code": "err = parse_line(line, outline, &outlen, args, &arg,\n                 PARSE_OPT_ENV | PARSE_OPT_DQUOTE | PARSE_OPT_SQUOTE |\n                 PARSE_OPT_BKSLASH | PARSE_OPT_SHARP, &errptr);",
      "note": "Source. After `readcfgfile()` reads and trims the current attacker-controlled configuration line, this tokenizer call is the first project parser statement on the crashing path that materializes the vulnerability-relevant state: the token buffer extent `outlen`, the token pointers in `args[]`, and the error bits later tested by the bad alert branch.",
      "kind": "source"
    },
    {
      "file": "src/cfgparse.c",
      "function": "readcfgfile",
      "line": null,
      "code": "if (err & PARSE_ERR_TOOMANY) {\n    ha_alert(\"parsing [%s:%d]: too many words, truncating at word %d, position %ld: <%s>.\\n\",\n             file, linenum, MAX_LINE_ARGS, (long)(args[MAX_LINE_ARGS-1] - outline + 1), args[MAX_LINE_ARGS-1]);\n    err_code |= ERR_ALERT | ERR_FATAL;\n    fatal++;\n    goto next_line;\n}",
      "note": "Root cause. When `parse_line()` returns both `PARSE_ERR_TOOMANY` and `PARSE_ERR_TOOLARGE`, `readcfgfile()` takes this branch before the later `if (err & (PARSE_ERR_TOOLARGE|PARSE_ERR_OVERLAP))` block that would resize `outline` to `outlen`. It therefore computes and formats `args[MAX_LINE_ARGS-1]` while that pointer still lies beyond the current `outline` allocation. The staged patch fixes the bug by moving this whole `PARSE_ERR_TOOMANY` block below the allocation-retry branch.",
      "kind": "root_cause_function"
    },
    {
      "file": "src/cfgparse.c",
      "function": "readcfgfile",
      "line": 1977,
      "code": "if (err & PARSE_ERR_TOOMANY) {\n    ha_alert(\"parsing [%s:%d]: too many words, truncating at word %d, position %ld: <%s>.\\n\",\n             file, linenum, MAX_LINE_ARGS, (long)(args[MAX_LINE_ARGS-1] - outline + 1), args[MAX_LINE_ARGS-1]);\n    err_code |= ERR_ALERT | ERR_FATAL;\n    fatal++;\n    goto next_line;\n}",
      "note": "Root cause. When `parse_line()` returns both `PARSE_ERR_TOOMANY` and `PARSE_ERR_TOOLARGE`, `readcfgfile()` takes this branch before the later `if (err & (PARSE_ERR_TOOLARGE|PARSE_ERR_OVERLAP))` block that would resize `outline` to `outlen`. It therefore computes and formats `args[MAX_LINE_ARGS-1]` while that pointer still lies beyond the current `outline` allocation. The staged patch fixes the bug by moving this whole `PARSE_ERR_TOOMANY` block below the allocation-retry branch.",
      "kind": "root_cause_line"
    },
